workaround:
Add a channel pin to `sentry.server.config.mjs` (works before or after `Sentry.init()`, harmless on Node), then run with `bun --preload ./sentry.server.config.mjs dist/server/entry.mjs`:

    import * as Sentry from '@sentry/astro';
    import * as dc from 'node:diagnostics_channel';

    if (!Sentry.isInitialized()) {
      Sentry.init({ dsn: '...', tracesSampleRate: 1.0 });
    }

    // Bun drops diagnostics_channel subscriptions at the next GC; holding the
    // channel objects keeps Sentry's subscriptions alive.
    globalThis.__sentryPinnedChannels ??= [
      'mysql2:query', 'mysql2:execute', 'mysql2:connect', 'mysql2:pool:connect',
      'orchestrion:mysql:query', 'orchestrion:mysql2:query', 'orchestrion:mysql2:execute',
    ].map(name => dc.tracingChannel(name));

For a driver-agnostic version, retain every channel instead of listing names (must go in a file preloaded before `Sentry.init()`, and must patch the CJS module object — the ESM namespace is read-only on bun):

    import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
    const dc = createRequire(import.meta.url)('node:diagnostics_channel');
    const kept = (globalThis.__keptChannels = []);
    const [oc, ot] = [dc.channel, dc.tracingChannel];
    dc.channel = n => { const c = oc(n); kept.push(c); return c; };
    dc.tracingChannel = n => { const t = ot(n); kept.push(t); return t; };
    await import('./sentry.server.config.mjs');

Two, both verified end to end (span with correct parent + `sentry-trace` and `baggage` on the wire), on both traceLifecycle 'static' and 'stream'.

A) Write a ~20-line integration from public @sentry/core exports (preferred; no cross-runtime package). File fetch-integration.js next to sentry.server.config.js:

  import { addFetchInstrumentationHandler, defineIntegration, getClient, instrumentFetchRequest,
           isSentryRequestUrl, LRUMap, shouldPropagateTraceForUrl } from '@sentry/core';
  export const denoFetchIntegration = defineIntegration(() => {
    const headersUrlMap = new LRUMap(100);
    const spans = {};
    return { name: 'DenoFetch', setupOnce() {
      addFetchInstrumentationHandler(handlerData => {
        const client = getClient();
        if (!client || isSentryRequestUrl(handlerData.fetchData.url, client)) return;
        instrumentFetchRequest(handlerData, () => true,
          url => shouldPropagateTraceForUrl(url, client.getOptions().tracePropagationTargets, headersUrlMap),
          spans, { spanOrigin: 'auto.http.fetch' });
      });
    }};
  });

then in sentry.server.config.js: `integrations: [denoFetchIntegration()]`. All seven core exports are present in the shipped @sentry/core 10.67.0 build. Produces `http.client` with origin `auto.http.fetch`.

B) One-liner, if pulling an extra package is acceptable: `import { fetchIntegration } from '@sentry/cloudflare';` and `integrations: [fetchIntegration()]`. It is pure globalThis.fetch patching with no Workers-only APIs, so it loads and runs fine under Deno inside the Astro server bundle. Same span and headers, plus fetch breadcrumbs.

C) If you only need the trace to continue and not the span: use `node:http`/`node:https` for the outgoing call (fully instrumented on Deno, verified in every run), or wrap the fetch in `Sentry.startSpan(...)` and attach headers from `Sentry.getTraceData()` yourself, which is what the repo's own deno e2e test does.

Real fix belongs in the SDK: port the `fetchIntegration` that @sentry/bun and @sentry/cloudflare already have into @sentry/deno, and/or have @sentry/node fall back to globalThis.fetch patching when the undici diagnostics channels never fire.

User-land workaround, verified end to end: pass an empty `_metadata` object to `Sentry.init`, which makes the spread in `init` share the object reference so the elysia metadata written onto `userOptions` also lands on `options`.

  Sentry.init({
    dsn: '...',
    tracesSampleRate: 1.0,
    _metadata: {},
  } as any);

Result (probe file app-metadata-fix.ts, static lifecycle):
  CLIENT_SDK_METADATA={"sdk":{"name":"sentry.javascript.elysia","packages":[{"name":"npm:@sentry/elysia","version":"10.67.0"},{"name":"npm:@sentry/bun","version":"10.67.0"}],"version":"10.67.0"}}
  transaction | env=sentry.javascript.elysia@10.67.0 | pkgs=npm:@sentry/elysia,npm:@sentry/bun | GET /users/:id (http.server)
  event       | env=sentry.javascript.elysia@10.67.0 | pkgs=npm:@sentry/elysia,npm:@sentry/bun | sentinel

`_metadata` is an internal option and needs a TypeScript cast, so this is a stopgap for someone blocked on SDK-name-based routing or quotas, not something to put in a guide. The real fix is the one-line reorder in packages/elysia/src/sdk.ts (move `applySdkMetadata` above the spread, as @sentry/bun and @sentry/hono already do, or call it on `options`).

details:
REFUTED. An `http.client` span for a native outgoing `fetch` does appear on Deno, in three of the four wirings I ran. The original probe only ever tested `traceLifecycle: 'static'` with an unwrapped `fetch`, which is the exact one combination that produces headers-without-a-span.

Root cause (packages/core/src/fetch.ts:106-116, `instrumentFetchRequest`):
  const hasParent = !!getActiveSpan();
  const shouldEmitSpan = hasParent || (!!client && hasSpanStreamingEnabled(client));
  const span = shouldCreateSpanResult && shouldEmitSpan ? startInactiveSpan(...) : new SentryNonRecordingSpan();
So the span is dropped only when the lifecycle is `static` AND there is no active span at the fetch call site. Wiring (A) hits both: `traceLifecycle: 'static'` plus withElysia's Node branch, which (with no Deno.serve root span) leaves the handler body running outside any active span. Change either half and the span appears.

--- (A) @sentry/elysia, LIFECYCLE=stream (the default on develop). http.client span present.
