workaround:
Two, both user-land, both verified.

Best (fixes events AND transactions, keeps the SDK's request metadata): a first middleware that scrubs the isolation scope it inherited. In app/main.ts, run this before `ctx.next()` (FRESH_ISO=2 in my probe, `deno serve -A --port 41541 _fresh/server.js`):

  const iso = Sentry.getIsolationScope();
  const d = iso.getScopeData();
  for (const k of Object.keys(d.tags ?? {})) iso.setTag(k, undefined);
  for (const k of Object.keys(d.contexts ?? {})) iso.setContext(k, null);
  for (const k of Object.keys(d.extra ?? {})) iso.setExtra(k, undefined);
  iso.setUser(null);
  iso.clearBreadcrumbs();
  iso.clearAttachments();

Result on the same sequence that leaked above:
  tx    | GET /api/iso | tags= {"probe":"first"}
  event | probe-msg | tags= {} | user= undefined | ctx= undefined
  tx    | GET /api/msg | tags= {}
  tx    | GET /api/dyn | tags= {"probe":"DYN"}
  event | probe-msg | tags= {} | user= undefined
  tx    | GET /api/iso | tags= {"probe":"second"}
  event | sentinel-10 | tags= {} | user= undefined | ctx= undefined

Do NOT use the obvious `Sentry.withIsolationScope(new Sentry.Scope(), ...)` middleware (FRESH_ISO=1). It cleans events but silently misattributes transactions, because `Sentry.setTag` then writes to the user's scope while the transaction is still built from the SDK's leaked one:
  tx    | GET /api/iso | tags= undefined        <- probe=first lost
  tx    | GET /api/msg | tags= {"probe":"first"} <- and applied to the next request instead

Second, partial: keep Fresh in dev, or preload every route module at boot so no `import()` ever runs inside a request. That only shrinks the blast radius to one poisoned request per route; any other in-request dynamic import re-opens it.

Register a user-land fetch tracing integration; it is a straight port of packages/cloudflare/src/integrations/fetch.ts and needs `"@sentry/core": "npm:@sentry/core@10.67.0"` in the deno.json import map. app/fetch-tracing.ts:

import type { Span } from "@sentry/core";
import { addFetchInstrumentationHandler, defineIntegration, getClient, instrumentFetchRequest, isSentryRequestUrl } from "@sentry/core";
const spans: Record<string, Span> = {};
export const fetchTracingIntegration = defineIntegration(() => ({
  name: "FetchTracing",
  setup() {
    addFetchInstrumentationHandler(handlerData => {
      const client = getClient();
      if (!client || isSentryRequestUrl(handlerData.fetchData.url, client)) return;
      instrumentFetchRequest(handlerData, () => true, () => true, spans, { spanOrigin: "auto.http.deno.fetch" });
    });
  },
}));

then `Sentry.init({ ..., integrations: [fetchTracingIntegration()] })` in instrument.ts. The `isSentryRequestUrl` guard is required, otherwise the SDK's own envelope requests get spans. Verified in vite dev and in the `deno serve _fresh/server.js` build, under both traceLifecycle static and stream. Alternative with no SDK-internal APIs: call the service with `node:http`/`node:https` instead of `fetch`, which denoHttpIntegration already instruments end to end (span + sentry-trace + baggage), as run A shows.

In instrument.ts, after Sentry.init, install an OTel ContextManager that maps the OTel active-span key to the active Sentry root span - then Fresh 2 names its own routes, in dev and in the build, in both lifecycles, with no Fresh middleware:

const SPAN_KEY = Symbol.for("OpenTelemetry Context Key SPAN");
function bridgeSpan() {
  const active = Sentry.getActiveSpan();
  if (!active) return undefined;
  const root = Sentry.getRootSpan(active);
  return {
    updateName(n) { Sentry.updateSpanName(root, n); return this; },
    setAttribute(k, v) {
      root.setAttribute(k, v);
      if (k === "http.route") root.setAttribute("sentry.segment.name.source", "route");
      return this;
    },
    setAttributes(a) { for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(a)) this.setAttribute(k, v); return this; },
    spanContext: () => root.spanContext(),
    isRecording: () => true,
    setStatus() { return this; },
    recordException(e) { Sentry.captureException(e); return this; },
    addEvent() { return this; },
    end() {},
  };
}
class Ctx {
  constructor(m = new Map()) { this.m = m; }
  getValue(k) { return k === SPAN_KEY ? bridgeSpan() : this.m.get(k); }
  setValue(k, v) { const m = new Map(this.m); m.set(k, v); return new Ctx(m); }
  deleteValue(k) { const m = new Map(this.m); m.delete(k); return new Ctx(m); }
}
context.setGlobalContextManager({
  active: () => new Ctx(),
  with: (_c, fn, self, ...a) => fn.call(self, ...a),
  bind: (_c, t) => t,
  enable() { return this; },
  disable() { return this; },
});

(`context` from "@opentelemetry/api", which must be in deno.json imports.) Errors still need the try/catch middleware from the earlier report - this bridge only fixes naming. The alternative, if you already have a Fresh middleware, is the reported `Sentry.updateSpanName(root, `${ctx.req.method} ${ctx.route}`)` + `http.route` + `sentry.segment.name.source` middleware; it produces the same names.

Two, both verified. (1) No bundler plugin at all — keep the plain `wrangler dev`/deploy and module-scope routes, and rename the request span from a Hapi ext (run2/routes.mjs):

  server.ext('onPreResponse', (request, h) => {
    const active = Sentry.getActiveSpan();
    const root = active ? Sentry.getRootSpan(active) : undefined;
    if (root && request.route?.path) {
      Sentry.updateSpanName(root, `${request.method.toUpperCase()} ${request.route.path}`);
      root.setAttribute(Sentry.SENTRY_SEGMENT_NAME_SOURCE, 'route');
    }
    return h.continue;
  });

`request.route.path` is Hapi's parameterized pattern, so the name stays low cardinality. `updateSpanName` alone would set the source to `custom`; the extra `SENTRY_SEGMENT_NAME_SOURCE` attribute (exported from @sentry/cloudflare) makes it `route`. Works on both traceLifecycle values. It gives no `router` child span.

(2) Automatic naming without Vite — pre-bundle with esbuild and `sentryOrchestrionPlugin` from `@sentry/server-utils/orchestrion/esbuild` (bundle:true, format:'esm', platform:'node', external:['cloudflare:*'], plus a `createRequire` banner), and hang that build off wrangler's `build.command` so `npx wrangler dev`/`wrangler deploy` stays the only command. This yields `GET /users/{id}` with source=route plus a `router GET /users/{id}` child span, identical to the Vite build. With either bundler plugin, `server.route(...)` and `server.start()` must be deferred into the first fetch, otherwise the isolate fails to boot.

