ROOT CAUSE of the reported crash (reproduced, with the exact frame the reporter did not have). I rebuilt the reporter's module-scope shape (src2/index.js + vite.config.top.mjs + wrangler.top.jsonc) and got the identical failure:

  service core:user:v4_koa_cf_top: Uncaught Error: Disallowed operation called within global scope. Asynchronous I/O (ex: fetch() or connect()), setting a timeout, and generating random values are not allowed within global scope.
      at null.<anonymous> (index.js:54317:34) in use
      at null.<anonymous> (index.js:67832:5)

index.js:67832 is `app.use(router.routes()).use(router.allowedMethods());` and index.js:54317 is inside the injected Koa wrapper, in the `use` frame:

  if (!tr_ch_apm_hasSubscribers(tr_ch_apm$use)) return __apm$traced();
  return tr_ch_apm$use.start.runStores(__apm$ctx, () => { ... })   // <- line 54317

So `buildTimeInstrumentation` injects an `orchestrion:koa` channel around `Koa.prototype.use`, the Sentry subscriber starts a span there, span-id generation calls a random source, and workerd forbids random values at global scope. It never even reaches `app.listen`. This is why the reporter's mysql-free variant failed identically (it still had `app.use` at module scope) and why the crash disappeared with `buildTimeInstrumentation: false` (no channel, no subscriber, no span). Nothing about mysql or `ssr.noExternal` is involved. It is a real sharp edge: the instrumented `app.use` is what crashes, and the workerd error text points at Cloudflare's handler docs, not at Sentry, so it is very hard to attribute.

Not tested: `vite dev` (the sibling express probe found @cloudflare/vite-plugin's own dev runner crashes with `superCtor.prototype ... Received undefined`, unrelated to Sentry) and esbuild + orchestrion (express probe: worker will not boot, `Dynamic require of "tty"`).

workaround:
Two, depending on how much you want to change.

1. Full automatic capture (preferred, refutes the finding): build the Worker with Vite and add the Sentry plugin, then construct the Koa app lazily inside the first request instead of at module scope.

vite.config.mjs:
```js
import { cloudflare } from '@cloudflare/vite-plugin';
import { sentryCloudflareVitePlugin } from '@sentry/cloudflare/vite';
export default { plugins: [cloudflare(), sentryCloudflareVitePlugin()] };
```
src/index.js:
```js
import { httpServerHandler } from 'cloudflare:node';
import * as Sentry from '@sentry/cloudflare';
import Koa from 'koa';
import Router from '@koa/router';

let booted = false;
function boot() {
  if (booted) return;
  booted = true;
  const app = new Koa();
  const router = new Router();
  router.get('/users/:id', ctx => { ctx.body = { id: ctx.params.id }; });
  app.use(router.routes()).use(router.allowedMethods());
  app.listen(8080);                 // must run inside a handler, not at module scope
}
const inner = httpServerHandler({ port: 8080 });   // this one is fine at module scope

export default Sentry.withSentry(
  env => ({ dsn: '...', tracesSampleRate: 1.0 }),
  { fetch(request, env, ctx) { boot(); return inner.fetch(request, env, ctx); } },
);
```
`vite build`, then `wrangler dev --config dist/<worker>/wrangler.json`. Errors thrown in middleware/routes now arrive with `mechanism.type: 'auto.middleware.koa'`, `handled: false`, correct trace id and request data, and the transaction/server span is route-named. No `app.on('error')`.

2. If you cannot use Vite (hand-written entry + plain `wrangler dev`), the reporter's `app.on('error', err => Sentry.captureException(err, { mechanism: { type: 'koa.onerror', handled: false } }))` remains the only option — and there you also lose the `router` spans and the route-named transaction.

Do NOT do 1 with a module-scope `new Koa()` / `app.use()`: the injected `use` wrapper calls `diagnostics_channel` `runStores` there and workerd rejects it with "Disallowed operation called within global scope … at index.js:… in use", so the Worker never starts. `sentryCloudflareVitePlugin({ buildTimeInstrumentation: false })` avoids the crash but also removes the Koa instrumentation, so it is not a fix.

Not needed any more for route names. The working wiring: build the Worker with Vite (`plugins: [cloudflare(), sentryCloudflareVitePlugin()]`) and create the Koa app lazily inside the fetch handler instead of at module scope:

  import { httpServerHandler } from 'cloudflare:node';
  import * as Sentry from '@sentry/cloudflare';
  import { buildApp } from '../app.js';   // returns a NEW Koa app, does not listen

  let listening;
  const ensureApp = () => (listening ??= new Promise(r => buildApp().listen(8080, r)));
  const inner = httpServerHandler({ port: 8080 });

  export default Sentry.withSentry(
    env => ({ dsn: '...', tracesSampleRate: 1.0 }),
    { async fetch(request, env, ctx) { await ensureApp(); return inner.fetch(request, env, ctx); } },
  );

`app.on('error', err => Sentry.captureException(err, { mechanism: { type: 'koa.onerror', handled: false } }))` is still required for error events. The old manual naming (`Sentry.updateSpanName(Sentry.getRootSpan(Sentry.getActiveSpan()), `${ctx.method} ${ctx._matchedRoute}`)`) remains the only option if you cannot build through Vite, e.g. a plain `wrangler dev`/esbuild pipeline, since nothing injects the Koa channels there.

