Confirmed still true and unchanged: `@sentry/deno` exports no `setupKoaErrorHandler` (only `koaIntegration` / deprecated `denoKoaIntegration`), while `@sentry/node`, `@sentry/bun`, `@sentry/elysia`, `@sentry/astro`, and the serverless SDKs all do. That is a real, separate export gap — it just is not what blocks error capture, since the bundled path needs no manual call at all.

workaround:
Bundle the Koa app with esbuild and the Sentry orchestrion plugin, then run the bundle with Deno; no import hook, no manual `app.on('error')`.

build.mjs:
  import * as esbuild from 'esbuild';
  import { sentryOrchestrionPlugin } from '@sentry/server-utils/orchestrion/esbuild';
  await esbuild.build({
    entryPoints: ['./main.ts'],          // main.ts = import './instrument.ts'; import './app.ts';
    bundle: true, platform: 'node', format: 'esm', target: 'node22',
    outfile: 'dist/main.js',
    external: ['@sentry/deno', '@sentry/server-utils'],
    banner: { js: "import { createRequire as __cr } from 'node:module';\nconst require = __cr(import.meta.url);" },
    plugins: [sentryOrchestrionPlugin()],
  });

  node build.mjs
  deno run --no-prompt --allow-net --allow-env --allow-read --allow-sys dist/main.js

Both `external` entries and the banner are load-bearing (see details for the two failure modes). Without a bundler, the only unbundled fallback stays the hand-written `app.on('error', err => Sentry.captureException(err, { mechanism: { type: 'manual.koa.onerror', handled: false } }))`, which produces a correctly scoped event but the wrong mechanism and no koa spans or route names.

Register a fetch integration by hand in the app; `@sentry/deno` has none. In instrument.ts, before `Sentry.init`:

// fetch-integration.ts
import { addFetchInstrumentationHandler, defineIntegration, getClient, instrumentFetchRequest, isSentryRequestUrl } from "@sentry/core";
const spans: Record<string, any> = {};
export const manualFetchIntegration = defineIntegration(() => ({
  name: "ManualFetch",
  setupOnce() {
    addFetchInstrumentationHandler(handlerData => {
      const client = getClient();
      if (!client || isSentryRequestUrl(handlerData.fetchData.url, client)) return;
      instrumentFetchRequest(handlerData, () => true, () => true, spans, { spanOrigin: "auto.http.fetch" });
    });
  },
}));

then `Sentry.init({ ..., integrations: [manualFetchIntegration()] })`. `@sentry/core` must be an explicit dependency and mapped in deno.json's import map. This gives a correctly parented `http.client` span (origin `auto.http.fetch`) plus `sentry-trace`/`baggage` on the outgoing request, on both `traceLifecycle: 'static'` and `'stream'`, and it works with the Deno import hook OFF, which is the only mode in which Koa boots at all. Two lower-effort fallbacks if you do not want a custom integration: wrap the call in `Sentry.startSpan(...)` (gives a `manual`-origin span, still no headers), or attach headers yourself from `Sentry.getTraceData()`. Both are strictly worse. For anything that runs over `node:http`/`node:https` instead of `fetch`, no workaround is needed: `denoHttpIntegration` already produces the span and the headers.

Two changes to the reported wiring, both in user land, no SDK change:

1. preload.ts
   import "./json-fix.ts";
   import "@sentry/deno/import";

   json-fix.ts (works around denoland/deno#36240: with any `node:module` hook registered, Deno compiles CJS `require('*.json')` as JS):
   import { registerHooks } from "node:module";
   import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
   import { fileURLToPath } from "node:url";
   registerHooks({
     load(url, context, nextLoad) {
       if (url.endsWith(".json")) {
         return { format: "json", source: readFileSync(fileURLToPath(url), "utf8"), shortCircuit: true };
       }
       return nextLoad(url, context);
     },
   });

2. Load it with the flag, not as an entry import:
   deno run --no-prompt --allow-net --allow-env --allow-read --allow-sys --import=./preload.ts main.ts
   where main.ts is just `import "./instrument.ts"; import "./app.ts";`
   (Equivalent alternative: keep the imports in main.ts but make the app load dynamic, `import "./json-fix.ts"; import "@sentry/deno/import"; await import("./instrument.ts"); await import("./app.ts");`)

With this, `http.route` and the `GET /users/:id` name with source `route` appear in both `stream` and `static` lifecycles, Koa `router` spans appear, and the Koa auto error handler works, so the manual `app.on("error", ...)` from the reported wiring can be dropped.

There is no unbundled workaround; the bundle is the only path. Minimal working recipe (verified, build-b.ts):

import { sentryBunPlugin } from '@sentry/bun/plugin';
await Bun.build({
  entrypoints: ['./src/main.ts'], outdir: './dist', target: 'bun', sourcemap: 'external',
  external: ['@sentry/bun', 'mysql',
             '@nestjs/microservices', '@nestjs/microservices/microservices-module',
             '@nestjs/websockets', '@nestjs/websockets/socket-module',
             '@nestjs/platform-express/multer', 'class-validator', 'class-transformer', 'cache-manager'],
  plugins: [sentryBunPlugin()],
});

then `bun run build.ts && bun dist/main.js`. `@sentry/bun` and the instrumented drivers may be listed in `external` (the plugin un-externalizes instrumented packages itself); the six @nestjs optional-peer entries plus class-validator/class-transformer/cache-manager must be listed or the build fails to resolve them. src/main.ts must start with `import './instrument';` then `import 'reflect-metadata';`, instrument.ts must init `@sentry/bun`, app.module.ts needs `SentryModule.forRoot()` plus `{ provide: APP_FILTER, useClass: SentryGlobalFilter }`, and tsconfig.json needs experimentalDecorators + emitDecoratorMetadata. Do NOT try `Bun.plugin(sentryBunPlugin())` at runtime: it breaks @nestjs/common's CommonJS named exports and the app will not boot.

