workaround:
Register one Oak middleware before `router.routes()` that renames the root span in a `finally` block and sets the segment name source with the exported constant, not the legacy `sentry.source` string:

app.use(async (ctx, next) => {
  try {
    await next();
  } finally {
    const path = ctx.matched?.[ctx.matched.length - 1]?.path;
    const span = Sentry.getActiveSpan();
    if (path && span) {
      const root = Sentry.getRootSpan(span);
      const routeName = `${ctx.request.method} ${path}`;
      Sentry.updateSpanName(root, routeName);
      root.setAttribute(Sentry.SENTRY_SEGMENT_NAME_SOURCE, 'route');
      root.setAttribute('http.route', path);
      Sentry.getIsolationScope().setTransactionName(routeName);
    }
  }
});

This yields `GET /users/:id` with source `route` under both `traceLifecycle: 'static'` and `'stream'`, and it also runs on the throwing route, which the `await next()`-then-rename form does not.

Mount with `Deno.serve(app.fetch)` and add one app-level middleware that names the span in a `finally` block, so it also runs when the handler throws:

app.use(async (ctx, next) => {
  try {
    await next();
  } finally {
    const span = Sentry.getActiveSpan();
    const route = ctx.matched?.at(-1)?.path;
    if (span && route) {
      Sentry.updateSpanName(span, `${ctx.request.method} ${route}`);
      span.setAttribute(Sentry.SENTRY_SEGMENT_NAME_SOURCE, 'route');
      span.setAttribute('http.route', route);
    }
  }
});

Register it before `app.use(router.routes())`. It works in both `traceLifecycle: 'stream'` and `'static'`, on success and error paths, and with prefixed routers (`/api/users/:id`). A router-level `router.use((ctx, next) => { ...; return next(); })` is equivalent. Under `app.listen()` there is no workaround, `getActiveSpan()` is undefined there.

Install `@sentry/bun` next to `@sentry/react-router` (same version, deduped to one `@sentry/core`) and add its `bunHttpServerIntegration()` to the server-side `Sentry.init()`:

```js
// instrument.server.mjs — imported as the very first line of server.js
import * as Sentry from '@sentry/react-router';
import { bunHttpServerIntegration } from '@sentry/bun';

Sentry.init({
  dsn: process.env.SENTRY_DSN,
  tracesSampleRate: 1.0,
  integrations: [bunHttpServerIntegration()],
});
```

That single line restores incoming trace continuation (trace_id + parent_span_id from `sentry-trace`/`baggage`), per-request isolation scopes, request/IP data on events, and an `http.server` root span, under both `traceLifecycle: 'static'` and the default `'stream'`. It works for plain Express routes too, so it does not depend on React Router's instrumentation API. It does NOT bring back library spans (express layers, db, AI) — those still need a Bun bundle built with `sentryBunPlugin()`.

A better long-term fix inside the SDK would be for `@sentry/node`'s `httpServerIntegration` (or the framework SDKs built on it) to fall back to the `http.Server.prototype.emit` patch when `process.versions.bun` is set, so no extra package and no extra integration are needed.

