(D) WORKS — the reported fix, wrapRequestHandler + SentryCF.setAsyncLocalStorageAsyncContextStrategy() at module scope: each event carries only its own tag, own URL, own trace id.

No regression from switching to withSentry: GET /boom/7 still yields {"type":"event","exception":"remix-cf loader error 7","url":".../boom/7","trace":"843aadefd24249eba377b9f8421b451b"} plus {"type":"transaction","name":"GET /boom/:id","source":"route","status":"internal_error","spans":["function routes/boom.$id"]} on the same trace (entry.server.tsx keeps `export const handleError = sentryHandleError`).

Root cause confirmed in source, and it is narrower than "the SDK is broken": packages/cloudflare/src/request.ts routes through wrapRequestHandlerWithInit -> withInvocationIsolationScope (packages/cloudflare/src/utils/invocationScope.ts), and that file's own JSDoc says the stack fallback "does not fork ... there the fork degrades to a no-op". The /request entry deliberately omits setAsyncLocalStorageAsyncContextStrategy because it is the entry for runtimes that CANNOT enable nodejs_compat (its JSDoc names Shopify Oxygen), and the strategy needs node:async_hooks. So on plain Workers with nodejs_compat the fix is to use the documented Workers entry (withSentry), not /request. The repo's own e2e app dev-packages/e2e-tests/test-applications/remix-hydrogen/server.ts uses the wrapRequestHandler shape without the strategy call, so a Hydrogen/Oxygen app is still exposed; @sentry/remix/cloudflare does not re-export setAsyncLocalStorageAsyncContextStrategy (build/esm/cloudflare/index.js), so an Oxygen user must import the @sentry/cloudflare main entry to get it.

workaround:
On Cloudflare Workers (nodejs_compat available), do not use wrapRequestHandler from @sentry/cloudflare/request for a Remix worker entry. Use the main entry instead:

import { createRequestHandler } from '@remix-run/cloudflare';
import * as Sentry from '@sentry/cloudflare';
import { instrumentBuild } from '@sentry/remix/cloudflare';
import * as remixBuild from './build/server/index.js';

const handleRequest = createRequestHandler(instrumentBuild(remixBuild), 'production');

export default Sentry.withSentry(
  () => ({ dsn: '...', tracesSampleRate: 1.0 }),
  { async fetch(request, env, ctx) { return handleRequest(request, { env, ctx }); } },
);

Keep `export const handleError = sentryHandleError` in app/entry.server.tsx. Works on both traceLifecycle 'static' and the default 'stream'.

If you must keep wrapRequestHandler (Hydrogen on Oxygen, or any shape already built on @sentry/cloudflare/request) and the runtime does have AsyncLocalStorage, add at module scope of the worker entry:
import * as SentryCF from '@sentry/cloudflare';
SentryCF.setAsyncLocalStorageAsyncContextStrategy();
That also fixes it, at the cost of importing the main entry.

Keep `import './instrument.server.mjs'` as the first line of the server entry and instead of relying on the runtime module hook, bundle the entry with build-time channel injection and switch the hook off:

// instrument.server.mjs
import * as Sentry from '@sentry/remix';
Sentry.init({ dsn, tracesSampleRate: 1.0, traceLifecycle: 'static' | 'stream', enableRuntimeChannelInjection: false });

// build-server.mjs (run under node, after `npx remix vite:build`)
import * as esbuild from 'esbuild';
import { sentryOrchestrionPlugin } from '@sentry/server-utils/orchestrion/esbuild';
await esbuild.build({
  entryPoints: ['./server.mjs'], outfile: './dist-server/server.mjs',
  bundle: true, platform: 'node', format: 'esm', target: 'node20',
  external: ['@sentry/*', 'mysql2', 'react', 'react-dom', 'react-dom/server'],
  banner: { js: "import { createRequire as __sentryCreateRequire } from 'node:module';\nconst require = __sentryCreateRequire(import.meta.url);" },
  plugins: [sentryOrchestrionPlugin()],
});

Run: `deno run --no-prompt --allow-net --allow-env --allow-read --allow-sys dist-server/server.mjs`.
`@remix-run/server-runtime` must be bundled (not external) — that is where the transform lands; mysql2 may stay external because 3.20+ publishes its own diagnostics channels, and @sentry/* must stay external. The old workaround (import the driver before instrument, no bundler) still works but is no longer the only option.

details:
REFUTED. db spans work on bun with the documented SolidStart 2 wiring plus a 5-line pin in the app. The original diagnosis is wrong on both counts: build-time injection DOES happen, and no Bun-plugin build step is needed.

Probe dir: $SCRATCH/deep/verify4-M-solidstart-bun-dbspans/ (app/ is the docs-shaped SolidStart 2 app; the vite.config.ts matches ~/dev/sentry-worktrees/audit-runtime-docs/dev-packages/e2e-tests/test-applications/solidstart-2/vite.config.ts one-for-one).

=== 1. The exact change (app-side, no SDK patch) ===
In app/server/plugins/sentry.ts, right after Sentry.init():

  import dc from 'node:diagnostics_channel';
  (globalThis as Record<string, unknown>).__sentryPinnedChannels = [
    'mysql2:query', 'mysql2:execute', 'mysql2:connect', 'mysql2:pool:connect',
    'orchestrion:mysql:query', 'orchestrion:mysql2:query', 'orchestrion:mysql2:execute',
  ].map(name => dc.tracingChannel(name));

A/B on the IDENTICAL build (`npx vite build`, then `PORT=<p> SENTRY_TUNNEL=http://127.0.0.1:45123/ SENTRY_LIFECYCLE=static [PIN_CHANNELS=1] bun .output/server/index.mjs`), sink lines verbatim:

  --- NO PIN (vrun-h-final-nopin.log):
  {"n":"GET /api/db","spans":["middleware GET /api/db","http.server GET /api/db"]}
  --- PIN (vrun-i-final-pin.log):
  {"n":"GET /api/db","spans":["middleware GET /api/db","http.server GET /api/db","db mysql2.pool.connect","db mysql2.connect","db SELECT id, name FROM users"]}

Same for the `mysql` (v2.x) driver, which needs orchestrion build-time injection rather than mysql2's own channels (vrun-e-bun-pin-static-both.log):
  {"type":"transaction","name":"GET /api/db-mysql1","spans":["middleware GET /api/db-mysql1","http.server GET /api/db-mysql1","db SELECT id, name FROM users"]}

Works on both lifecycles. traceLifecycle 'stream' with the pin (vrun-f-bun-pin-stream.log):
  {"t":"span","items":["middleware GET /api/db","db mysql2.pool.connect","db mysql2.connect","db SELECT users","http.server GET","http.server GET","middleware GET /api/sentinel"]}

=== 2. Root cause: bun drops diagnostics_channel subscriptions on GC ===
Not an injection problem. deep/verify4-M-solidstart-bun-dbspans/dctest3.mjs subscribes three ways without keeping a reference to the channel object, forces GC, then publishes:

  $ bun dctest3.mjs   -> {"runtime":"bun","plainSubscribe":0,"channelSubscribe":0,"tracing":0}
  $ node --expose-gc dctest3.mjs -> {"runtime":"node","plainSubscribe":1,"channelSubscribe":1,"tracing":1}

bun 1.4.0 collects a channel that still has subscribers when nothing else holds it; node keeps subscribed channels alive. The SDK subscribes eagerly on Bun (packages/server-utils/src/orchestrion/instrumentation.ts: "Bun and Deno have no such channel limit ... there we just subscribe immediately") and then discards the channel: subscribeMysql2DiagnosticChannels -> bindTracingChannelToSpan(tracingChannel(name), ...) throws away the returned handle (packages/server-utils/src/integrations/mysql2/mysql2-dc-subscriber.ts:93-126, packages/server-utils/src/tracing-channel.ts:120,200). Between the nitro plugin's Sentry.init() and the first request bun GCs, so the subscription is gone before mysql2 publishes. This is generic: every channel-based integration on bun is exposed, not just db.

=== 3. The SDK-side one-liner also fixes it, with zero app changes ===
Patched node_modules/@sentry/server-utils/build/esm/tracing-channel.js to retain what it subscribes to (`const __SENTRY_RETAINED_CHANNELS__ = []` + `__SENTRY_RETAINED_CHANNELS__.push(handle.channel, channel)` after `handle.channel.subscribe(subscribers)`), rebuilt, ran WITHOUT the app pin (vrun-g-bun-sdkretain-nopin.log):
  {"t":"transaction","n":"GET /api/db","spans":["middleware GET /api/db","http.server GET /api/db","db mysql2.pool.connect","db mysql2.connect","db SELECT id, name FROM users"]}
  {"t":"transaction","n":"GET /api/db-mysql1","spans":["middleware GET /api/db-mysql1","http.server GET /api/db-mysql1","db SELECT id, name FROM users"]}
The patch was reverted afterwards (original kept at tracing-channel.orig.js) and the final .output is the unpatched baseline, so a re-run reproduces the broken/fixed A/B above.

=== 4. What the original report got wrong ===
- "mysql2 is externalized by nitro, so build-time injection never sees it" — false. The stock build bundles and transforms it: .output/server/_ssr/promise-4o3jIiz1.mjs contains `dc.tracingChannel("mysql2:query")` (x5 channels) and `globalThis.__SENTRY_ORCHESTRION_INJECT__ = orchestrionModuleInjected("mysql2", mysql2Integration)`. The `mysql` v2 driver chunk .output/server/_ssr/db-mysql1-*.mjs contains `orchestrion:mysql:query`. No `ssr.noExternal` needed.
- "[Sentry] Bundler plugin ran, injected libraries=[]" is a red herring: mysql2 >= 3.20 is a `registrationOnly` orchestrion entry (packages/server-utils/src/orchestrion/config/mysql2.ts) and publishes its own channels, so an empty list says nothing about whether db spans can work.
- "No available Node API to register diagnostics-channel injection hooks" is irrelevant here — the runtime hook is only a second path to injection, and the bundler already did the job.

=== 5. Not tested ===
bun 1.3.14 (the CI pin at $S/bun1314 no longer exists on disk), so I cannot say whether this is a bun 1.4.0 regression.

Commands to rerun: `PORT=45123 node $S/qs/sink.mjs &`; `cd app && npx vite build`; `PORT=45131 SENTRY_TUNNEL=http://127.0.0.1:45123/ SENTRY_LIFECYCLE=static bun .output/server/index.mjs` (no db spans) vs the same with `PIN_CHANNELS=1` (db spans); `curl http://127.0.0.1:45123/__items`.

Reproduced the report exactly, found the root cause in the build output, and made the broken half work with a 6-line user-side middleware. The other half (SSR page render errors) stays swallowed even then.

Setup (same as the report): @solidjs/start 2.0.2 + nitro 3.0.260610-beta + vite 8, develop SDK tarballs, `sentrySolidStart()` in vite.config.ts, `Sentry.init` in `server/plugins/sentry.ts`, `sentryBeforeResponseMiddleware()` in `src/middleware.ts`, `npx vite build` then `bun .output/server/index.mjs`. Sink on 41777, app on 41778, sentinel = `GET /api/sentinel` (captureMessage + flush) after every probe request, so absence is deterministic.

RUN A - baseline, docs wiring, bun, traceLifecycle 'static' ($S/deep/verify4-M-solidstart-bun-errors/run-a-baseline.log). `src/routes/api/boom.ts` = `export function GET(){ throw new Error('probe-api-route-error') }`. Confirmed swallowed:
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /api/boom","op":"http.server","source":"url","status":"internal_error","spans":["middleware GET /api/boom","http.server GET /api/boom"]}
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /api/boom-async","op":"http.server","source":"url","status":"internal_error",...}
{"type":"event","exception":"Error: Error thrown from Solid Start E2E test app server route","mechanism":"auto.function.solidstart","handled":false,...}
{"type":"event","message":"sentinel","mechanism":"generic","handled":true,...}
No `event` for either boom route, before the sentinel. Async throws behave identically to sync ones.

ROOT CAUSE (read from `.output/server/`, not guessed). SolidStart 2 builds its SSR renderer as its own Nitro service with its own h3 app: `_ssr/ssr.mjs` ends with `const app = new H3(); app.use(handler); return app;` - constructed with NO config, so `config.onError` is undefined. h3's `prepareResponse` then does `if (error.unhandled && !config.silent) console.error(error)` and, with no `onError`, returns `errorResponse(...)` directly. The 500 Response is handed back to the outer Nitro app as a normal response, so the main app's `onError` in `.output/server/index.mjs` line 350 (`createH3App({ onError(error, event){ captureError(error,{event}); ... } })`) - the thing that calls `hooks.callHook("error", ...)`, i.e. what `@sentry/nitro`'s server plugin hooks - never runs. A previous run of this same probe registered an extra `nitroApp.hooks.hook('error', ...)` by hand and its `[probe] nitro error hook fired` line never printed (`run-hook.log`), which matches.

PROOF the Nitro side is fine and only the SolidStart service swallows - RUN C ($V/run-c-nitroroute.log): added `server/routes/nitro-boom.ts` = `defineHandler(() => { throw new Error('probe-nitro-route-error') })`, no user error handling at all:
{"type":"event","exception":"Error: probe-nitro-route-error","mechanism":"auto.http.nitro.onTraceError","handled":false,"hasContextLine":true}
{"type":"event","exception":"Error: probe-nitro-route-error","mechanism":"chained","handled":true,...}
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /nitro-boom","op":"http.server","source":"route","status":"internal_error",...}
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /api/boom","op":"http.server","source":"url","status":"internal_error",...}   <- still no event
(Side observation, not this capability: the Nitro route error is reported twice, once as `auto.http.nitro.onTraceError` handled=false and once as `chained` handled=true.)

RUN B - the fix, bun, 'static' ($V/run-b-mw.log). `src/middleware.ts` wraps `next()` (see workaround). Both the sync and the async API-route throw now arrive:
{"type":"event","exception":"Error: probe-api-route-error","mechanism":"solidstart.middleware","handled":false,"sdk":"sentry.javascript.solidstart","runtime":"node","hasContextLine":true}
{"type":"event","exception":"Error: probe-api-route-error-async","mechanism":"solidstart.middleware","handled":false,...,"hasContextLine":true}
{"type":"event","exception":"Error: Error thrown from Solid Start E2E test app server route","mechanism":"auto.function.solidstart","handled":false,...}
plus the three `internal_error` transactions and the sentinel.

RUN D - same fix, bun, traceLifecycle 'stream' ($V/run-d-mw-stream.log): works, error event plus `{"type":"span",...,"raw":[{"name":"GET /api/boom","origin":"auto.http.nitro.h3"},{"name":"GET","status":"error","is_segment":true,"origin":"auto.http.nitro.srvx"}]}`.

RUN E - same fix, node instead of bun ($V/run-e-mw-node.log): works and is actually richer, the event carries request data and the transaction name, which it does not on bun:
{"type":"event","exception":"Error: probe-api-route-error","mechanism":"solidstart.middleware","handled":false,"transaction":"GET /api/boom","request":{"url":"http://127.0.0.1:41778/api/boom","method":"GET",...}}
So the swallow is a SolidStart 2 / nitro issue, not a bun regression, and the fix is runtime-independent - matching the original report's node cross-check.

RUN F - alternative fix, `entry-server.tsx` keeps the handler and sets `handler.config.onError` ($V/run-f-onerror.log). `createHandler()` returns the h3 app instance (`StartHandler extends H3`), so this reaches the SSR service directly. It fires (`[probe] h3 onError fired probe-api-route-error`) and delivers an event, but by then h3 has already wrapped the throw in an `HTTPError`, so the reported exception chain is `HTTPError` with the real error as a `chained` cause (`{"type":"event","exception":"Error: probe-api-route-error","mechanism":"chained","handled":true,...}` - values[0] is the cause). Grouping would key on `HTTPError`. The middleware is the better shape.

RUN G - remaining gap ($V/run-g-mw-page.log). A component that throws during SSR (`src/routes/page-error.tsx`, `export default function PageError(){ throw new Error('probe-page-render-error') }`) is NOT rescued by the middleware: Solid's renderer catches it inside the render, so `next()` resolves and the middleware sees no throw. Only the transaction arrives:
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /page-error","op":"http.server","source":"url","status":"internal_error","spans":["middleware GET /page-error","http.server GET /page-error"]}
{"type":"event","exception":"Error: probe-api-route-error","mechanism":"solidstart.middleware","handled":false,...}   <- the API route in the same run
No event for the page render error, before the sentinel. That surface stays dark with every variant I tried.

REFERENCE APP: `~/dev/sentry-worktrees/audit-runtime-docs/dev-packages/e2e-tests/test-applications/solidstart-2/` has no `src/routes/api/**` route and no server-render-throw route at all - its only server error test is the `withServerActionInstrumentation` page. So the SDK's own e2e suite does not cover the case that is broken.

Files (all rerunnable, `node_modules` removed after the run):
- app: $SCRATCH/deep/verify4-M-solidstart-bun-errors/app
  (src/middleware.ts, src/entry-server.tsx, src/routes/api/{boom,boom-async,ok,sentinel}.ts, src/routes/page-error.tsx, src/routes/server-error.tsx, server/plugins/sentry.ts, server/routes/nitro-boom.ts, vite.config.ts)
  Both fixes are gated by env vars so one build covers every variant: `PROBE_MW=1` (middleware) and `PROBE_ONERROR=1` (h3 config.onError).
- Rerun: `cd app && npm install --legacy-peer-deps && npx vite build`; `PORT=41777 node $S/qs/sink.mjs &`; `SENTRY_TUNNEL=http://127.0.0.1:41777/ SENTRY_LIFECYCLE=static PORT=41778 [PROBE_MW=1] bun .output/server/index.mjs`; then `curl 127.0.0.1:41778/api/boom`, `curl 127.0.0.1:41778/api/sentinel`, `curl 127.0.0.1:41777/__items`.
- A stray `app/app/` directory is a copy I made by mistake at the start; nothing reads it (no `rm` allowed).

workaround:
Add an h3 middleware that wraps `next()` in `src/middleware.ts`. `createMiddleware` accepts a raw h3 `Middleware[]` in SolidStart 2, so this is a supported shape and composes with the Sentry meta-tag middleware:

```ts
// src/middleware.ts
import * as Sentry from '@sentry/solidstart';
import { createMiddleware } from '@solidjs/start/middleware';

export default createMiddleware([
  async (event, next) => {
    try {
      return await next();
    } catch (error) {
      Sentry.captureException(error, { mechanism: { handled: false, type: 'solidstart.middleware' } });
      throw error;
    }
  },
  ...createMiddleware({ onBeforeResponse: [Sentry.sentryBeforeResponseMiddleware()] }),
]);
```

This catches sync and async throws from `src/routes/**/*.ts` file API routes on both bun and node, in both `traceLifecycle` modes, with context lines and `handled: false`, and it keeps the original error as the top-level exception. It does not catch errors thrown while a page component renders on the server.

Alternative that reaches the same h3 app but reports worse: in `entry-server.tsx`, keep the value from `createHandler(...)` and set `handler.config.onError = error => Sentry.captureException(error, { mechanism: { handled: false, type: '...' } })`. h3 has already wrapped the throw in an `HTTPError` at that point, so the issue groups as `HTTPError` with the real error attached as a chained cause.

details:
REFUTED. The reported diagnosis is right about the mechanism but wrong that nothing can fix it: the SDK already ships the fix, `bunHttpServerIntegration()` from `@sentry/bun`, and adding it to the existing `Sentry.init()` restores incoming-trace continuation (and per-request isolation) to full node parity, with no change to the build, the preset, or the entry shape.

ROOT CAUSE, isolated (probe $SCRATCH/deep/verify4-M-solidstart-bun-incomingtrace/dc-probe.mjs):
  node dc-probe.mjs -> {"runtime":"node","hits":["start:/probe","finish"],"hasTracingChannel":true}
  bun  dc-probe.mjs -> {"runtime":"bun","hits":[],"hasTracingChannel":true}
Bun 1.4.0's `node:http` never publishes the `http.server.request.start` diagnostics channel that `@sentry/node`'s `httpServerIntegration` subscribes to (packages/core/src/integrations/http/constants.ts:3, packages/node/src/integrations/http/httpServerIntegration.ts `wrapServerEmitRequest`, which is where `propagation.extract` + the isolation scope happen). It is the node:http path that matters here, not Bun.serve: the nitro `node-server` preset resolves `srvx` through the `node` export condition at build time, so `.output/server/_libs/srvx.mjs` starts with `import "node:http"` and contains zero `Bun.serve` references even when run under bun. The nitro srvx/h3 tracing channels (packages/nitro/src/runtime/hooks/captureTracingEvents.ts) really do only `startInactiveSpan`, so with no continuation upstream every request lands on the process-global propagation context.

A. BASELINE, docs-shaped wiring, `bun .output/server/index.mjs` — broken, reproduces the report:
  {"activeTraceId":"19e8973040fe45ae95550b998fd043f4","rootTraceId":"19e8973040fe45ae95550b998fd043f4","rootParentSpanId":null,...}   <- request WITH sentry-trace 12345678901234567890123456789012-1234567890abcdef-1
  {"activeTraceId":"19e8973040fe45ae95550b998fd043f4",...}                                                                            <- request WITHOUT headers, same trace id
  {"type":"transaction","name":"GET /api/hello","op":"http.server","source":"url","status":"ok","trace_id":"19e8973040fe45ae95550b998fd043f4","sdk":"sentry.javascript.solidstart","spans":["middleware GET /api/hello","http.server GET /api/hello"],"hasRequestData":false}
  (all three transactions + the sentinel event share 19e897…, none has parent_span_id, no request data)

B. NODE CONTROL, same build, `node .output/server/index.mjs` — works:
  {"type":"transaction","name":"GET /api/hello",...,"trace_id":"12345678901234567890123456789012","parent_span_id":"1234567890abcdef","spans":["http.server GET /api/hello","middleware GET /api/hello","http.server GET /api/hello"]}

C. FIX — same node-server build, same `bun .output/server/index.mjs`, only `integrations: [bunHttpServerIntegration()]` added to the nitro plugin's `Sentry.init()`:
  {"activeTraceId":"12345678901234567890123456789012","rootTraceId":"12345678901234567890123456789012","rootParentSpanId":"1234567890abcdef",...}
  {"activeTraceId":"67d2da44e2614a79bcdac051ee6fbb86",...}   <- unheadered request gets its own trace, isolation restored
  {"type":"transaction","name":"GET /api/hello","op":"http.server","source":"url","status":"ok","trace_id":"12345678901234567890123456789012","parent_span_id":"1234567890abcdef","sdk":"sentry.javascript.solidstart","spans":["http.server GET /api/hello","middleware GET /api/hello","http.server GET /api/hello"],"hasRequestData":false}
  {"type":"event","message":"sentinel",...,"transaction":"GET /api/sentinel","trace_id":"81934bfcacab4cb0a75d0ce6c0f9c39a","user":{"ip_address":"127.0.0.1"},"request":{"url":"http://127.0.0.1:47317/api/sentinel","method":"GET",...}}
  Span tree, transaction naming, request data and user ip are byte-for-byte the same shape as the node control (B).

D. FIX under `traceLifecycle: 'stream'` — also works; the segment span carries the incoming parent:
  {"type":"span","trace_id":"12345678901234567890123456789012","items":["middleware GET /api/hello","http.server GET","http.server GET","http.server GET"],"raw":[...,{"name":"GET","status":"ok","parent_span_id":"1234567890abcdef","is_segment":true,"origin":"auto.http.server"}]}

E. `bunHttpServerIntegration({ spans: false })` — also continues the trace and isolates, but drops the node:http server span, leaving only the two nitro channel spans. Prefer the default (`spans: true`), which matches node exactly.

F. Alternative that also works but is worse: build with the nitro `bun` preset (`NITRO_PRESET=bun npx vite build`, which flips srvx to its `Bun.serve` adapter) plus `bunServerIntegration()` from `@sentry/bun`. Trace continuation works (`"trace_id":"12345678901234567890123456789012","parent_span_id":"1234567890abcdef"`), but the extra root span is unparameterized (`spans:["http.server GET /","middleware GET /api/hello","http.server GET /api/hello"]`) and the bun preset alone, without the integration, is still broken (all requests on 8cbbbda65e2a4f479e36538da34c8de3, transaction names collapse to "GET /"). Do not recommend this path.

Probe files: $SCRATCH/deep/verify4-M-solidstart-bun-incomingtrace/ — app/ (vite.config.ts, server/plugins/sentry.ts with a PROBE_INT switch, src/middleware.ts, src/routes/api/hello.ts returning the live trace ids, src/routes/api/sentinel.ts), probe.sh (starts the server, sends one headered + one unheadered request, sends the sentinel, waits for it in the sink, dumps items), dc-probe.mjs, log-*.txt.
Rerun: `cd app && npm install --legacy-peer-deps && npx vite build` (node_modules was removed after the run; `.output` is current and self-contained), then `PORT=47311 node $S/qs/sink.mjs &` and `SINK_PORT=47311 APP_PORT=47316 ./probe.sh <label> env PROBE_INT=bunhttp-spans bun .output/server/index.mjs`. PROBE_INT values: unset (baseline), bunhttp (`{spans:false}`), bunhttp-spans (default), bunserve. SENTRY_LIFECYCLE=stream switches lifecycle.
No SolidStart or bun page in ~/dev/sentry-docs mentions `bunHttpServerIntegration`; grep over docs/platforms/javascript/guides/solidstart/ and platform-includes finds no occurrence, and no bun mention at all in the SolidStart guide.

workaround:
Add `@sentry/bun` as a dependency and pass its `bunHttpServerIntegration()` to the existing `Sentry.init()` in `server/plugins/sentry.ts`. Nothing else changes — same `vite build`, same nitro node output, same `bun .output/server/index.mjs`:

  import { bunHttpServerIntegration } from '@sentry/bun';
  import * as Sentry from '@sentry/solidstart';
  import { definePlugin } from 'nitro';

  export default definePlugin(() => {
    Sentry.init({
      dsn: '…',
      tracesSampleRate: 1.0,
      integrations: [bunHttpServerIntegration()],
    });
  });

The integration self-disables on Node (`if (!process.versions.bun) return`), so the same source builds and runs unchanged under `node .output/server/index.mjs` — verified in run B, where the import was present. Cost: `@sentry/bun` adds roughly 2.4 MB to the server bundle.

details:
REFUTED. The reported breakage reproduces exactly with the docs-shaped wiring, and it is fixed by one line in the nitro plugin: adding `bunHttpServerIntegration()` from `@sentry/bun` to the `Sentry.init()` options. `@sentry/bun` on develop ships this integration precisely for this gap and its own `init()` enables it by default; `@sentry/solidstart` (which calls `@sentry/node`'s `init`) does not.

Root cause confirmed at the source: server-side request isolation lives only in `httpServerIntegration`, which does `subscribe(HTTP_ON_SERVER_REQUEST, ...)` on the `http.server.request.start` node:http diagnostics channel (`packages/node/src/integrations/http/httpServerIntegration.ts:151`, channel name at `packages/core/src/integrations/http/constants.ts:3`). Bun does not publish that channel, so `instrumentServer()` (`packages/core/src/integrations/http/server-subscription.ts:96`) — the code that clones the isolation scope and calls `continueTrace` — never runs. `@sentry/nitro`'s h3/srvx `dc.tracingChannel` subscribers do fire on bun, which is why spans exist, but they only call `startInactiveSpan` and never fork isolation. `packages/bun/src/integrations/bunHttpServer.ts:88-95` documents the same diagnosis and closes it by patching `http.Server.prototype.emit` and handing the server to the same core instrumentation.

EXACT CHANGE (server/plugins/sentry.ts):
  import { bunHttpServerIntegration } from '@sentry/bun';
  import * as Sentry from '@sentry/solidstart';
  import { definePlugin } from 'nitro';
  export default definePlugin(() => {
    Sentry.init({ dsn, tunnel, tracesSampleRate: 1.0, traceLifecycle: 'static',
      integrations: [bunHttpServerIntegration()] });
  });
plus `npm install $S/pack/sentry-bun-10.67.0.tgz` alongside the other tarballs. Nothing else changes: same vite.config.ts, same `src/middleware.ts`, same `npx vite build`, same `bun .output/server/index.mjs`.

