workaround:
Two small pieces of user code restore parameterized names on both runtimes and BOTH lifecycles. Verified end to end.

1) API routes — one global SolidStart middleware. `event.params` is populated by SolidStart before `onBeforeResponse`, so the pattern can be rebuilt from it:

// src/middleware.ts  (register with solidStart({ middleware: './src/middleware.ts' }))
import { setHttpServerSpanRouteAttribute } from '@sentry/server-utils';
import { createMiddleware } from '@solidjs/start/middleware';
export default createMiddleware({
  onBeforeResponse: [(event) => {
    const params = event.params;
    if (!params || !Object.keys(params).length) return;
    const byValue = new Map(Object.entries(params).map(([k, v]) => [String(v), k]));
    const pattern = new URL(event.request.url).pathname.split('/')
      .map(s => (byValue.has(s) ? `:${byValue.get(s)}` : s)).join('/');
    setHttpServerSpanRouteAttribute(pattern);
  }],
});

2) SSR page routes — call the same helper from the Router root during SSR, where `useCurrentMatches()` knows the pattern:

// src/app.tsx
import { setHttpServerSpanRouteAttribute } from '@sentry/server-utils';
import { Router, useCurrentMatches } from '@solidjs/router';
import { isServer } from 'solid-js/web';
function Root(props) {
  const matches = useCurrentMatches();
  if (isServer) {
    const last = matches()[matches().length - 1];
    const pattern = last?.route?.pattern || last?.route?.originalPath;
    if (pattern) setHttpServerSpanRouteAttribute(pattern);
  }
  return <Suspense>{props.children}</Suspense>;
}

Results with both in place — static lifecycle:
  {"name":"GET /api/users/:id","op":"http.server","source":"route","spans":["http.server GET /api/users/42","middleware GET /api/users/42","http.server GET /api/users/42"]}
  {"name":"GET /users/:id","source":"route"}
stream lifecycle (segment spans):
  {"name":"GET /api/users/:id","op":"http.server","src":"route","route":"/api/users/:id","seg":true}
  {"name":"GET /users/:id","op":"http.server","src":"route","route":"/users/:id","seg":true}

Notes and cheaper variants:
- `setHttpServerSpanRouteAttribute` is exported from `@sentry/server-utils` (a transitive dep of `@sentry/solidstart`); it is NOT re-exported from `@sentry/solidstart`, so the app has to depend on `@sentry/server-utils` directly. Making `@sentry/solidstart` re-export it would be a one-line SDK improvement.
- Per-route variant, no middleware: call `setHttpServerSpanRouteAttribute('/api/users/:id')` at the top of the handler -> `{"name":"GET /api/w1/:id","source":"route"}` (route `w1` in the probe).
- Without `@sentry/server-utils`, `getRootSpan(getActiveSpan())` + `updateSpanName(root, 'GET /api/x/:id')` + `root.setAttribute('http.route', ...)` also renames, but leaves `source: "custom"` rather than `"route"` (route `w2`: `{"name":"GET /api/w2/:id","source":"custom"}`), because it does not set `sentry.segment.name.source`.
- Mutating `getRequestEvent().nativeEvent.context.matchedRoute = { route: '/api/x/:id' }` works too (route `w3`, `source: "route"`), since `@sentry/nitro`'s `beforeSpanEnd` re-reads it — but it pokes an h3 internal, so prefer the helper.
- Only the segment span is renamed; the two inner non-segment `http.server` spans stay `"GET"` under stream lifecycle. That is cosmetic and does not affect transaction grouping.
- Moving an endpoint out of `src/routes/` into a real Nitro route under `server/` (with `nitro({ serverDir: './server' })`) gets parameterized names with zero Sentry code, if that fits the app.

The real fix belongs in the SDK: `@sentry/solidstart` should hook SolidStart's own matcher (it already computes `"path": "/api/users/:id"` in `matchAPIRoute`, and page matches are available via the router) and call `setHttpServerSpanRouteAttribute`, the same way `@sentry/nitro` does for h3 routes.

In `src/instrumentation.server.ts`, install `@sentry/bun` alongside `@sentry/sveltekit` and pass its fetch integration into `Sentry.init`, gated on the runtime so node builds do not get a duplicate span:

```ts
import { fetchIntegration } from '@sentry/bun';
import * as Sentry from '@sentry/sveltekit';

Sentry.init({
  dsn: '...',
  tracesSampleRate: 1.0,
  integrations: typeof globalThis.Bun !== 'undefined' ? [fetchIntegration()] : [],
});
```

Verified on bun 1.4.0 with both `traceLifecycle: 'static'` and the default `'stream'`: the `http.client` span appears under the `http.server` segment and the outgoing request carries `sentry-trace` and `baggage` with the server transaction's trace id.

details:
Reproduced the reported behavior exactly, with the documented wiring, and could not refute it. Probe dir: $SCRATCH/deep/verify4-M-sveltekit-cf-routenames/ (app/, logs bB1-bB5.log builds, wB1-wB5.log wrangler, sinkB.log). Same stack as reported: SvelteKit 2.70.3 / svelte 5.57.0 / adapter-cloudflare 5.1.0 / vite 6.4.3, @sentry/sveltekit 10.67.0 from $S/pack, wrangler 4.129.0, compatibility_date 2026-05-01 + nodejs_compat. Sink on 37421, worker on 37422.

Commands: (cd app && npm install --no-audit --no-fund) ; PORT=37421 node $S/qs/sink.mjs & ; (cd app && npx vite build && npx wrangler dev --port 37422 --inspector-port 0) ; curl -s http://127.0.0.1:37422/users/42 ; curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:37422/sentinel?n=N" ; curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:37421/__items?raw=1".

VARIANT 1 - docs wiring exactly as in ~/dev/sentry-docs/platform-includes/getting-started-complete/javascript.sveltekit.mdx:331-352 (sequence(initCloudflareSentryHandle({...}), sentryHandle())), stream lifecycle (develop default). Segment loses the route:
  {"name": "GET /users/[id]", "is_segment": false, "attrs": {"sentry.origin": "auto.http.sveltekit", "sentry.op": "http.server", "url.path": "/users/42", "http.route": "/users/[id]"}}
  {"name": "GET", "is_segment": true, "attrs": {"sentry.origin": "auto.http.cloudflare", "sentry.op": "http.server", "sentry.segment.name.source": "url", "url.path": "/users/42"}}
Worse than reported: this is not limited to parameterized routes. A plain static route loses its name too, because the Cloudflare wrapper only keeps a name whose source is already 'route' (i.e. only "/"):
  {"name": "GET /sentinel", "is_segment": false, ...auto.http.sveltekit...}
  {"name": "GET", "is_segment": true, "attrs": {"sentry.origin": "auto.http.cloudflare", "sentry.segment.name.source": "url", "url.path": "/sentinel"}}

VARIANT 2 - same wiring, traceLifecycle: 'static'. Segment carries the raw URL:
  TX GET /users/42 {"source": "url"} {"sentry.origin": "auto.http.cloudflare", "sentry.op": "http.server", "sentry.segment.name.source": "url", "url.path": "/users/42"}
    span GET /users/[id] {"sentry.origin": "auto.http.sveltekit", "http.route": "/users/[id]"}
  TX GET /sentinel {"source": "url"} ... span GET /sentinel ...

VARIANT 3 - SvelteKit native tracing (svelte.config.js kit.experimental.tracing.server = true), stream lifecycle. This makes it strictly WORSE, not better: sentryHandle skips its own span (kitTracingEnabled), Kit's root span never materializes on Cloudflare, and no route-named http.server span exists at all - the route survives only as an attribute on a `function` span:
  {"name": "sveltekit.resolve", "is_segment": false, "attrs": {"sentry.origin": "auto.http.sveltekit", "http.route": "/users/[id]", "sentry.op": "function"}}
  {"name": "sveltekit.handle.sequenced.sentryRequestHandler", "is_segment": false, "attrs": {"sentry.origin": "auto.function.sveltekit.handle", "sentry.op": "function"}}
  {"name": "GET", "is_segment": true, "attrs": {"sentry.origin": "auto.http.cloudflare", "sentry.op": "http.server", "sentry.segment.name.source": "url", "url.path": "/users/42"}}
The SDK already knows about this: packages/sveltekit/src/server-common/integrations/svelteKitSpans.ts has "in Cloudflare, the kit-emitted root span is missing but our cloudflare SDK emits the http.server span".

ROOT CAUSE (read in the worktree, not guessed). packages/cloudflare/src/wrapRequestHandlerWithInit.ts creates the segment span and comments "After the handler runs, the span name might have been updated by nested instrumentation (e.g., Remix parameterizing routes)" - i.e. the contract is that the framework layer renames the SEGMENT. packages/sveltekit/src/server-common/handle.ts does not: outside Kit tracing it unconditionally `startSpan(...)`s a NEW child with the route name/http.route, and inside Kit tracing it only renames `event.tracing.root`, which on Cloudflare is not the segment (and here does not exist). Nothing in @sentry/cloudflare or @sentry/sveltekit ever calls getRootSpan()/updateSpanName() on the Cloudflare segment.

TEST COVERAGE GAP: dev-packages/e2e-tests/test-applications/sveltekit-3-cloudflare-workers/tests/server.test.ts asserts the segment name is "GET /" for the "/" route. "/" is the one path whose URL-derived name already has source 'route', so the assertion passes while every other route is broken. The app has no parameterized route.

Errors are unaffected: the captured event still carries the right grouping name, because sentryHandle sets it on the isolation scope - {"type":"event","message":"sentinel-1","transaction":"GET /sentinel","sdk":"sentry.javascript.cloudflare"}. Only span/transaction naming is wrong.

workaround:
A user-land `handle` inserted between `initCloudflareSentryHandle` and `sentryHandle` fixes both lifecycles. Verified working (file: app/src/hooks.server.workaround.ts in the probe dir):

  import { getActiveSpan, getRootSpan, handleErrorWithSentry, initCloudflareSentryHandle, sentryHandle } from '@sentry/sveltekit';
  import { sequence } from '@sveltejs/kit/hooks';

  const nameSegmentByRoute = ({ event, resolve }) => {
    const routeId = event.route?.id;
    const activeSpan = getActiveSpan();
    if (routeId && activeSpan && !event.isSubRequest) {
      const segment = getRootSpan(activeSpan);
      segment.updateName(`${event.request.method} ${routeId}`);
      segment.setAttributes({ 'sentry.segment.name.source': 'route', 'http.route': routeId });
    }
    return resolve(event);
  };

  export const handle = sequence(initCloudflareSentryHandle({ ... }), nameSegmentByRoute, sentryHandle());

Do NOT use Sentry.updateSpanName() here - it stamps `sentry.segment.name.source: 'custom'`; setting 'route' explicitly is what makes Sentry treat it as a parameterized route. The `!event.isSubRequest` guard keeps a same-origin `fetch` from a server `load` from renaming the outer segment.

Stream lifecycle after the workaround:
  {"name": "GET /users/[id]", "is_segment": true, "attrs": {"sentry.origin": "auto.http.cloudflare", "sentry.op": "http.server", "sentry.segment.name.source": "route", "url.path": "/users/42", "http.route": "/users/[id]"}}
Static lifecycle after the workaround:
  TX GET /users/[id] {"source": "route"} {"sentry.origin": "auto.http.cloudflare", "sentry.segment.name.source": "route", "http.route": "/users/[id]", "url.path": "/users/42"}
    span GET /users/[id] {"sentry.origin": "auto.http.sveltekit", "http.route": "/users/[id]"}

The proper SDK fix is the same three lines moved into `instrumentHandle` in packages/sveltekit/src/server-common/handle.ts: when the active span's root is not the span sentryHandle owns (the Cloudflare wrapper's segment), rename that root instead of, or in addition to, starting the child.

Prime mysql2's package.json in the CJS require cache before Sentry's module hooks are installed. Either add a preload file and start the server with it (no app change):
  // prewarm-json.mjs
  import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
  createRequire(import.meta.url)('mysql2/package.json');
  HOST=127.0.0.1 PORT=<p> deno run --no-prompt --allow-net --allow-env --allow-read --allow-sys --allow-ffi --preload ./prewarm-json.mjs build/index.js
or put the same two lines at the very top of src/hooks.server.js, above the `@sentry/sveltekit` import and `Sentry.init`. The `mysql` (v2) driver needs no workaround at all. Both give a `db` span parented under the SvelteKit `http.server` span, in `traceLifecycle: 'stream'` and `'static'`.

details:
Reproduced exactly, then found a one-line fix that fully repairs it — so the finding stands for the documented wiring, and the gap is a missing integration, not a runtime limitation.

Setup: SvelteKit 2.60 + adapter-node 5 + svelte 5 + vite 6, @sentry/sveltekit 10.67.0 (develop tarballs), `vite build` under node 26.5.0, run under deno 2.9.0 (aarch64-apple-darwin). hooks.server.js does `Sentry.init({ dsn, tunnel, tracesSampleRate: 1.0, traceLifecycle })` at module top plus `sentryHandle()` / `handleErrorWithSentry()`. Sink on 51101, recorder on 51102. Probe dir: $SCRATCH/deep/verify4-M-sveltekit-deno-outgoingfetch/ (node_modules removed at the end; `npm install --no-audit --no-fund` recreates it, then `npx vite build`).

A) BASELINE, documented wiring, traceLifecycle 'static' (run-b-app-baseline.log). Global `fetch()` -> no span; SvelteKit's own `event.fetch` -> no span either; `node:http` -> span, as reported:
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /fetchout","op":"http.server","source":"route","status":"ok","spans":[],"hasRequestData":false}
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /eventfetch","op":"http.server","source":"route","status":"ok","spans":[],"hasRequestData":false}
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /httpout","op":"http.server","source":"route","status":"ok","spans":["http.client GET http://127.0.0.1:51102/rec-http"],"hasRequestData":false}
recorder:
{"path":"/rec-fetch","sentryTrace":null,"baggage":null,"traceparent":null}
{"path":"/rec-eventfetch","sentryTrace":null,"baggage":null,"traceparent":null}
{"path":"/rec-http","sentryTrace":"69e303992a5c4eb4aa2e24cea0a62a16-9b363fae35f1e55e-1","baggage":"sentry-environment=production,...,sentry-transaction=GET%20%2Fhttpout,...","traceparent":null}
Command: `HOST=127.0.0.1 PORT=51110 SINK_PORT=51101 REC_PORT=51102 LIFECYCLE=static deno run --no-prompt --allow-net --allow-env --allow-read --allow-sys build/index.js`

Root cause confirmed in source, not guessed: @sentry/node's fetch instrumentation is purely a `diagnostics_channel` subscriber on `undici:request:create` / `undici:client:sendHeaders` / `undici:request:headers` / `undici:request:trailers` / `undici:request:error` (~/dev/sentry-worktrees/audit-runtime-docs/packages/node/src/integrations/node-fetch/undici-instrumentation.ts:107-113). Deno's `fetch` is native Rust, not undici, so nothing ever publishes on those channels. Grepping `addFetchInstrumentationHandler|instrumentFetchRequest` across packages/node/src and packages/sveltekit/src returns nothing: neither package patches `globalThis.fetch`, so no option or init placement can fix this. `traceLifecycle`, init placement, and hook shape are all irrelevant to it.

B) FIX THAT WORKS — add a global-fetch integration. Two variants, both verified end to end.

B1) Shipped SDK code, one line: `integrations: [fetchIntegration()]` imported from `@sentry/cloudflare` (packages/cloudflare/src/integrations/fetch.ts, the WinterCG-style `globalThis.fetch` patcher). traceLifecycle 'static' (run-b-app-cffix.log):
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /fetchout","op":"http.server","source":"route","status":"ok","spans":["http.client GET http://127.0.0.1:51102/rec-fetch"],"hasRequestData":false}
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /eventfetch","op":"http.server","source":"route","status":"ok","spans":["http.client GET http://127.0.0.1:51102/rec-eventfetch"],"hasRequestData":false}

B2) ~25-line userland integration built only from `@sentry/core` exports (`addFetchInstrumentationHandler`, `instrumentFetchRequest`, `isSentryRequestUrl`, `shouldPropagateTraceForUrl`, `LRUMap`, `defineIntegration`) — file src/userland-fetch-integration.js in the probe dir. traceLifecycle 'static' (run-b-app-userland.log):
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /fetchout","op":"http.server","source":"route","status":"ok","spans":["http.client GET http://127.0.0.1:51102/rec-fetch"],"hasRequestData":false}
{"type":"transaction","name":"GET /eventfetch","op":"http.server","source":"route","status":"ok","spans":["http.client GET http://127.0.0.1:51102/rec-eventfetch"],"hasRequestData":false}
recorder, both headers now on the wire:
{"path":"/rec-fetch","sentryTrace":"44c5cdb2c2d94017b55acbb98b9560b6-9dedde8fa70d6c7f-1","baggage":"sentry-environment=production,sentry-public_key=public,sentry-trace_id=44c5cdb2c2d94017b55acbb98b9560b6,sentry-transaction=GET%20%2Ffetchout,sentry-sampled=true,sentry-sample_rand=0.06871537103408998,sentry-sample_rate=1","traceparent":null}
{"path":"/rec-eventfetch","sentryTrace":"3c2f8274677543f5adb5e83442849b33-b4472cb93e1990de-1","baggage":"...sentry-transaction=GET%20%2Feventfetch,sentry-sampled=true,...","traceparent":null}
Also correct under the develop default traceLifecycle 'stream' (run-b-app-userland-stream.log) — child span, right parent, right origin, and the tunnel's own fetch is correctly excluded:
{"type":"span","trace_id":"af2f8d3fd49a4f678c65d8e56485e95e","items":["http.client GET 127.0.0.1","http.server GET /fetchout"],"raw":[{"name":"GET 127.0.0.1","status":"ok","parent_span_id":"a422d3032d1b7343","is_segment":false,"origin":"auto.http.fetch"},{"name":"GET /fetchout","status":"ok","is_segment":true,"origin":"auto.http.sveltekit"}]}

Variants tried and their outcome: traceLifecycle 'static' and 'stream' — no difference to the baseline break; SvelteKit's `event.fetch` instead of global `fetch` — same break (new data point the original probe did not have; it is worse than reported, since the framework's own fetch is affected too); init placement is fixed by SvelteKit (hooks.server.js top level) and already works, the SDK logs "SDK initialized from ESM"; the repo's own reference app dev-packages/e2e-tests/test-applications/sveltekit-2-svelte-5/src/hooks.server.ts uses exactly the wiring tested (init + sentryHandle + handleErrorWithSentry, no fetch integration), confirming there is nothing extra a user is supposed to add. Deno 2.8.3 (CI pin) not tested: $S/deno283 no longer exists on this machine.

Verdict is confirmed-broken rather than works-after-all because no documented wiring, option, init placement, lifecycle or bundler variant fixes it — the only fixes are adding an integration that no SvelteKit/Node/Deno docs page mentions, one of which lives in the wrong package (@sentry/cloudflare) and one of which the user has to write.

workaround:
Add to instrument.server.mjs, after Sentry.init (full file at .../verify4-M-tanstack-bun-dbspans/instrument.server.bun-fix.mjs):

  import diagnosticsChannel from 'node:diagnostics_channel';
  globalThis.__sentryKeepChannelsAlive = [
    'orchestrion:mysql:query',
    'orchestrion:mysql2:query',
    'orchestrion:mysql2:execute',
  ].map(name => diagnosticsChannel.tracingChannel(name));

then run the documented command with bun: `bun --preload ./.output/server/instrument.server.mjs .output/server/index.mjs`. Any strong reference works; keeping it on globalThis is what survives. Patching `dc.tracingChannel` from the preload does NOT work (v2-run-bun-keepall.log: "[v2] channels retained after init = 0") because the SDK's `import * as diagnosticsChannel` namespace does not see the patched property, so the channel names have to be listed. Real fix belongs in the SDK: retain the channel objects at the subscribe sites in packages/server-utils/src/integrations/* (or centrally for the Bun/Deno eager branch in packages/server-utils/src/orchestrion/instrumentation.ts), which would make this work with no user-side code.

Add a global-fetch integration in instrument.server.mjs, built from primitives @sentry/core already exports (this is a copy of @sentry/cloudflare's shipped `fetchIntegration`):

  import { addFetchInstrumentationHandler, defineIntegration, getClient, instrumentFetchRequest, isSentryRequestUrl, LRUMap, shouldPropagateTraceForUrl } from '@sentry/core';
  const denoFetchIntegration = defineIntegration(() => {
    const headersUrlMap = new LRUMap(100);
    const spans = {};
    return { name: 'DenoGlobalFetch', setupOnce() {
      addFetchInstrumentationHandler(handlerData => {
        const client = getClient();
        if (!client || isSentryRequestUrl(handlerData.fetchData.url, client)) return;
        instrumentFetchRequest(handlerData, () => true,
          url => shouldPropagateTraceForUrl(url, client.getOptions().tracePropagationTargets, headersUrlMap),
          spans, { spanOrigin: 'auto.http.fetch' });
      });
    }};
  });
  Sentry.init({ dsn, tunnel, tracesSampleRate: 1, integrations: [denoFetchIntegration()] });

Verified on Deno with both traceLifecycle 'static' and 'stream'. The proper SDK fix is for @sentry/node to fall back to this globalThis.fetch patching when the undici diagnostics channels never fire (non-undici runtimes: Deno, and Bun's native fetch), rather than leaving it to userland.

Separately, building this app at all needs a vite pre-plugin that externalizes `@sentry/*` in the non-client environments and gives each one a default export; without it `vite build` fails in rollup with "Cannot read properties of null (reading 'getVariableForExportName')" on node too. Full file at <probe>/vite.config.ts.

details:
REFUTED. Parameterized server transaction names work on Deno 2.9.0 with @sentry/tanstackstart-react 10.67.0 (develop). The reported mechanism is right (the `__SENTRY_ROUTE_PATTERNS__` build-time constant is only substituted while the SDK goes through Vite's transform pipeline) but the conclusion is wrong on two counts: (a) it is fixed by one line of vite.config.ts, no custom plugin needed; (b) it is not Deno-specific in either direction — the broken build config gives url names under node too, and the fixed build gives route names under both.

Exact change, in `vite.config.ts`, replacing the bare `nitro()` from the docs shape:

    import { nitro } from 'nitro/vite';
    ...
    nitro({ noExternals: ['@sentry/tanstackstart-react'] }),

That forces nitro to bundle the SDK into the server output instead of leaving it as a bare `@sentry/tanstackstart-react` import, so `sentry-tanstackstart-route-patterns` (packages/tanstackstart-react/src/vite/routePatterns.ts) actually sees `wrapFetchWithSentry` and inlines the array. Verified in the build output at
.output/server/_libs/sentry__tanstackstart-react.mjs:110 — `updateSpanWithRouteParametrization(method, url.pathname, ["/api/user/$id", ..., "/param/$id", "/users/$userId", ...])`.

A/B on the SAME app, same deno command, same sink. Deno 2.9.0, `PORT=<p> deno run --no-prompt --allow-net --allow-env --allow-read --allow-sys --allow-ffi deno-entry.mjs` where deno-entry.mjs is `import './instrument.server.mjs'; import './index.mjs';`.

